 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T12:59+08:00",
 "answer": "**Possibly a little better, but probably still unhealthy.** I can't see today's date, so I'm going by the latest NEA update I could find. That update was issued on 16 Oct.\n\n- **Forecast:** The PSI forecast for 17 Oct 2026 is 150 – 230 (Unhealthy to Very Unhealthy).\n- **Why it might ease:** Some showers are expected over Singapore and the surrounding region, including parts of Kalimantan. Winds are forecast to shift to blow from the southeast or south-southeast, which may reduce the transport of smoke haze from Kalimantan.\n- **Caveat:** Some smoke haze from Sumatra may drift towards Singapore.\n- **Latest readings:** On the morning of 17 Oct, the 24-hour PSI in central Singapore dipped back to the \"unhealthy\" band after staying in the \"very unhealthy\" range the day before. At 11am, it was in the \"unhealthy\" range in all regions: central (184), north (139), south (154), east (174), west (176).\n- **Outlook:** NEA also said showers are forecast over Singapore and parts of Kalimantan on Oct 18 and 19.\n\nThe haze looks to be easing slightly, but I wouldn't expect it to be clearly healthy yet. The PSI is a 24-hour average, so it lags behind changes in the air. If you're planning outdoor activities, check the 1-hour PM2.5 reading on the NEA website or the myENV app. At these PSI levels, NEA advises healthy people to avoid prolonged or strenuous outdoor exertion. It advises children, the elderly and pregnant women to minimise outdoor activity. People with chronic lung or heart disease should avoid outdoor activity.",
